// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["ix"] = {"qs": [["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "After an acquirer announces an open offer, another person announces a competing offer for the same target within the permitted time. What is the general effect?", ["The first offer is automatically cancelled", "Shareholders may choose either offer", "SEBI decides which offer shareholders must accept", "The target company must reject both offers"], 1, "SAST allows competing offers within a specified period. Both offers proceed on a common schedule, acquirers may revise their offers, and shareholders decide which to tender into."], ["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "Under SAST, the offer price in a mandatory open offer is broadly set as:", ["The highest of several benchmark prices specified in the regulations", "Any price the acquirer chooses, without reference to the market", "The face value of the shares", "The lowest price at which the stock traded in the last week"], 0, "The offer price must be at least the highest of benchmarks such as the negotiated price, the highest price paid by the acquirer in a look-back period and the volume-weighted average market price, protecting public shareholders."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "Which intermediary maintains the register of security holders and processes applications and allotment on behalf of an issuer?", ["Credit rating agency for the issuer's debt", "Registrar to an issue and share transfer agent", "Custodian of a foreign portfolio investor", "Debenture trustee for secured bonds"], 1, "The registrar (RTA) handles applications, finalises the basis of allotment with the exchange, credits securities and maintains holder records. Rating agencies rate debt, custodians hold assets for clients, and debenture trustees protect debenture holders."], ["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "An acquirer holds 40% of a listed company. Under the creeping acquisition route, what is the maximum holding it can reach by acquisitions in one financial year without an open offer?", ["45%", "50%", "42%", "75%"], 0, "An acquirer holding 25% or more but below the maximum permissible non-public shareholding may acquire up to 5% more in a financial year without an open offer: 40% + 5% = 45%."], ["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "A company transfers one of its divisions to a new company, whose shares are issued to the original company's shareholders. This is a:", ["Horizontal merger", "Buyback of shares", "Demerger", "Rights issue"], 2, "In a demerger, an undertaking is transferred to a resulting company, which issues shares to the demerged company's shareholders. It splits a business rather than combining two businesses."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "In the 'basis of allotment' for an oversubscribed retail portion of a main-board IPO, the general principle is to:", ["Allot shares only to the largest retail applicants first", "Allot shares in proportion to each applicant's annual income", "Give the minimum bid lot to as many applicants as possible", "Allot shares only to applicants who bid above the cut-off"], 2, "ICDR requires that, where possible, each retail applicant gets at least the minimum bid lot, with draw of lots if needed. Size priority or income has no role, and cut-off bids are valid retail bids."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "In project counselling, a merchant banker mainly helps a client with:", ["Collecting application forms from investors at bank branches", "Assessing project viability and preparing a financing plan", "Holding the client's shares in a depository account", "Clearing and settling the client's stock trades"], 1, "Project counselling covers feasibility studies, cost estimates, means of finance and preparing project reports for lenders. The other options are functions of bankers, depositories and clearing corporations."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "SEBI wants to examine whether a merchant banker's records comply with the regulations. Which power can it use?", ["Revocation of the company's incorporation certificate", "Freezing of the merchant banker's employees' salaries", "Inspection of the merchant banker's books and records", "Taking over the management of the issuer company"], 2, "SEBI can inspect a merchant banker's books, records and documents and follow up with directions or penalties. Company incorporation is under the Registrar of Companies, and SEBI does not take over issuer management in such cases."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "Which document contains the final issue price and number of securities after book building is completed?", ["Prospectus filed with the Registrar of Companies", "Draft red herring prospectus", "Abridged prospectus attached to the bid form", "Price band advertisement"], 0, "After bids close, the final price and issue size are included in the prospectus filed with the RoC. The DRHP and RHP do not contain the final price, and the price band advertisement only states the range."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "After the issue, investors complain of delays in unblocking funds for unallotted bids. Who is primarily responsible for coordinating the redress?", ["The stock exchange's listing department alone", "The lead manager, with the registrar and SCSBs", "The credit rating agency that rated the issuer", "SEBI's chairman personally"], 1, "The lead manager continues to coordinate post-issue activities, including refunds, unblocking and grievance redress, with the registrar and SCSBs. Rating agencies have no role, and SEBI oversees rather than handles each complaint."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "A merchant banker wants to lead manage an issue in which it holds a significant stake through an associate. Which concern does this mainly raise?", ["Insufficient number of employees for the issue", "A conflict of interest", "Failure to pay the stamp duty on the issue", "Breach of minimum public shareholding norms"], 1, "SEBI rules restrict a merchant banker from lead managing an issue of an associated entity because its objectivity in due diligence is compromised. The concern is conflict of interest, not staffing or public shareholding."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "A merchant banker underwrites ₹10 crore of a public issue. If the issue is undersubscribed and investors take up only ₹6 crore of that portion, the merchant banker must:", ["Return the ₹6 crore to the investors", "Ask SEBI to cancel the issue immediately", "Pay a penalty of ₹4 crore to the stock exchange", "Take up or procure the balance ₹4 crore"], 3, "Underwriting is a commitment to take up the shortfall. The underwriter must subscribe to, or arrange subscribers for, the unsubscribed ₹4 crore of its commitment. The issue is not automatically cancelled."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "In a book-built issue, the price band is announced and the floor price is ₹400. What is the maximum cap price permitted?", ["₹440", "₹480", "₹500", "₹420"], 1, "The cap of the price band cannot exceed 120% of the floor price, so the maximum cap = 1.2 × ₹400 = ₹480. ₹500 would be 125% of the floor and is not permitted."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "A company listed on the main board makes a further public issue of fresh shares. This is called a:", ["Follow-on public offer", "Initial public offer", "Qualified institutions placement to QIBs only", "Buyback of securities"], 0, "An FPO is a public issue by a company that is already listed. An IPO is the first public offer by an unlisted company, a QIP is restricted to QIBs, and a buyback returns money to shareholders."], ["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "An acquirer who already holds 30% of a target makes an open offer voluntarily to consolidate control. What is the minimum size of such a voluntary offer?", ["26% of the total shares", "5% of the total shares", "10% of the total shares", "51% of the total shares"], 2, "A voluntary open offer by an acquirer holding 25% or more must be for at least 10% of the total shares, without exceeding the maximum permissible non-public shareholding. The 26% minimum applies to mandatory offers."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "The minimum bid lot in a main-board IPO is designed so that the minimum application value falls:", ["Above ₹2 lakh, to exclude retail investors", "Within a band prescribed by SEBI", "At exactly ₹1 lakh for every issue", "At whatever value the anchor investors decide"], 1, "SEBI prescribes a band for the minimum application size so that small investors can participate. The issuer and lead manager set the lot within that band; it is not fixed at a single value or decided by anchors."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "A company can fund a buyback of its equity shares out of:", ["Proceeds of an earlier issue of the same kind of equity shares", "Free reserves, securities premium or other-kind issue proceeds", "Borrowed funds that take its debt to five times equity", "Money received as share application money pending allotment"], 1, "The Companies Act permits buyback out of free reserves, securities premium or proceeds of any shares or specified securities other than those of the same kind. Using proceeds of the same kind of shares is prohibited."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "Which method allows a listed company to raise capital by issuing shares only to qualified institutional buyers, without a full public offer?", ["Rights issue to all existing shareholders", "Follow-on public offer through book building", "Qualified Institutions Placement", "Bonus issue out of free reserves"], 2, "A QIP lets a listed company place equity or convertible securities with QIBs quickly, with lighter documentation than a public issue. A rights issue goes to existing holders and a bonus issue raises no fresh money."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "A monitoring agency appointed for an IPO reports that issue proceeds are being used for purposes not stated in the offer document. This deviation:", ["Must be disclosed, and a change in objects needs shareholder approval", "Can be ignored if the amount is below 50% of the issue", "Is permitted without any approval within three years", "Must be reported only to the company's auditors"], 0, "Proceeds must be used for stated objects. Any variation requires shareholders' approval by special resolution and appropriate disclosures, with exit options where applicable. Deviations cannot simply be ignored."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "A merchant banker advising a company on how to restructure its capital and raise funds is performing:", ["Corporate advisory services", "Depository participant services", "Stock broking services", "Registrar and transfer agent services"], 0, "Advising on capital structure, fund-raising options and restructuring is corporate advisory, a traditional merchant banking service. Depository, broking and RTA services are separate intermediary functions with their own registrations."], ["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "A merger in which two companies in the same industry and at the same stage of production combine is a:", ["Vertical merger", "Horizontal merger", "Conglomerate merger", "Reverse merger"], 1, "A horizontal merger joins competitors in the same line of business. A vertical merger joins firms at different stages of the supply chain, and a conglomerate merger joins unrelated businesses."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "Which of the following is a duty of the compliance officer of a merchant banker?", ["Deciding the price band of every issue managed by the firm", "Approving the issuer's annual financial statements", "Allotting shares to investors in every public issue", "Monitoring compliance and reporting lapses to SEBI"], 3, "The compliance officer monitors adherence to SEBI rules, circulars and regulations and must report any non-compliance observed to SEBI. Pricing, audit approval and allotment are handled by others."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "What happens to shares bought back by a company?", ["They are held as treasury shares and resold later", "They are transferred to the promoters free of cost", "They are given to employees as bonus shares", "They are extinguished within the prescribed time"], 3, "Shares bought back must be extinguished within the time prescribed; Indian law does not allow companies to hold them as treasury stock for resale. They cannot be passed on to promoters or employees."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "Units of the CPSE ETF and the Bharat 22 ETF, sold to investors by the government, represent which disinvestment method?", ["Strategic sale with transfer of control", "Buyback of shares by each CPSE", "Rights issue by the government", "Exchange traded fund route"], 3, "Through the ETF route, the government sells its holdings in a basket of CPSEs to an ETF, whose units investors buy. This divests stakes across many companies without transferring control, unlike a strategic sale."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "The main purpose of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956 is to:", ["Regulate the issue of bank notes and coins by the government", "Prevent undesirable transactions in securities", "Govern the incorporation and winding up of companies", "Regulate insurance and pension products sold to the public"], 1, "The SCRA regulates recognition of stock exchanges, contracts in securities and listing, to prevent undesirable transactions. Currency issue is under the RBI Act, company formation under the Companies Act, and insurance under the Insurance Act."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "Why is a depository important for a public issue in India?", ["It decides the price band for the issue", "It underwrites the unsubscribed portion of the issue", "Allotted shares are credited in demat form", "It grants SEBI's approval to the offer document"], 2, "Securities in public issues must be issued in dematerialised form, so depositories (NSDL and CDSL), through participants, credit allotted shares to investors. Pricing is with the issuer and lead manager, and SEBI issues observations."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "Which is an essential condition for SEBI to grant a certificate of registration to a merchant banker?", ["It meets the 'fit and proper person' criteria", "The applicant has already completed at least ten public issues", "The applicant is listed on a recognised stock exchange", "The applicant is owned by a public sector bank"], 0, "SEBI assesses integrity, reputation, competence and financial soundness under the fit and proper criteria. Prior issue experience, listing or public sector ownership are not preconditions for registration."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "Which step must occur before the red herring prospectus is filed with the Registrar of Companies?", ["Listing of the shares on the stock exchange", "Finalisation of the basis of allotment", "Receipt of SEBI's observations on the draft offer document", "Credit of shares to the investors' demat accounts"], 2, "The DRHP is filed with SEBI and the issue proceeds only after SEBI's observations are incorporated. Basis of allotment, credit of shares and listing all happen after the issue closes."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "An 'abridged prospectus' is:", ["A summary of salient features sent with the bid form", "A prospectus filed only with the stock exchange and not with SEBI", "A document issued only for debt securities", "The final prospectus after deletion of risk factors"], 0, "The abridged prospectus summarises key information and must accompany application forms so investors see the essentials. Risk factors cannot be dropped; it complements the full offer document."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "A company does not satisfy the track record conditions under regulation 6(1) and comes with an IPO under regulation 6(2). Which allocation rule applies?", ["At least 75% of the net offer must be allotted to retail investors", "At least 75% of the net offer must be allotted to QIBs", "At most 25% of the net offer can be allotted to QIBs", "The entire issue must be allotted to anchor investors"], 1, "Issuers coming under regulation 6(2) must allot at least 75% of the net offer to QIBs, with not more than 15% to NIIs and not more than 10% to retail investors, since such companies carry higher risk."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "While reviewing a draft offer document, the lead manager finds that a promoter director has been declared a wilful defaulter. What is the correct approach?", ["Ignore it, since only the company's own record matters", "Disclose it only after listing to avoid affecting demand", "Remove the director's name from the offer document", "Treat it as material, as such issuers face ICDR curbs"], 3, "ICDR restricts issuers whose promoters or directors are wilful defaulters or fugitive economic offenders from making public issues. The lead manager must treat this as material; hiding it or deferring disclosure would breach due diligence obligations."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "Which of the following is a reason a company may undertake a buyback?", ["To return surplus cash to shareholders", "To increase the number of shares outstanding", "To raise fresh capital for expansion", "To reduce its free reserves to zero by law"], 0, "Buybacks return excess cash, reduce share count and can raise EPS and return on equity. They reduce, not increase, shares outstanding and do not raise new capital."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "In a book-built IPO under regulation 6(1) of ICDR, what is the minimum share of the issue to be allocated to retail individual investors?", ["10%", "35%", "15%", "50%"], 1, "Under regulation 6(1), not less than 35% goes to retail investors, not less than 15% to non-institutional investors and not more than 50% to QIBs. 10% retail applies to issuers under regulation 6(2)."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "When must a lead manager file a due diligence certificate with SEBI in an IPO?", ["Only after the shares have listed", "Only if SEBI raises a query on the issue", "With the draft offer document and at later stages", "Only when the issue is undersubscribed"], 2, "Due diligence certificates are filed with the draft offer document and at specified later stages, such as filing with the RoC, confirming continued accuracy. They are not optional or triggered only by queries."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "A merchant banker is asked by a client to suppress adverse information from an offer document. What should it do?", ["Refuse, because full and true disclosure is its obligation", "Agree, provided the client pays a higher fee", "Agree, but mention the matter only to SEBI orally", "Agree if the information is less than one year old"], 0, "Merchant bankers must ensure the offer document contains all material information and must not suppress facts. Fee levels or informal disclosure to SEBI cannot justify misleading investors."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "Which of the following is an example of a qualified institutional buyer (QIB) under ICDR?", ["A resident individual with a large portfolio", "A Hindu Undivided Family investing ₹5 lakh", "A SEBI-registered mutual fund", "A partnership firm dealing in textiles"], 2, "QIBs include mutual funds, banks, insurers, pension funds, FPIs (other than certain categories) and similar institutions. Individuals, HUFs and ordinary partnership firms are not QIBs, regardless of investment size."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "Which of the following is the best description of the 'issue manager' role?", ["Holding the issue proceeds in an escrow account for the issuer", "Crediting allotted shares to investors' demat accounts", "Planning the issue and filing documents with SEBI", "Matching buy and sell orders on the exchange after listing"], 2, "The issue manager (lead manager) plans the issue, conducts due diligence, prepares the offer document, files it with SEBI and coordinates other intermediaries. Bankers hold funds, depositories credit shares and exchanges match trades."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "In which method of disinvestment does the government sell a significant stake along with management control to a buyer?", ["Strategic sale", "Offer for sale through stock exchange", "Exchange traded fund route", "Minority stake sale in an IPO"], 0, "A strategic sale transfers a substantial stake and management control to a strategic buyer. OFS, ETFs and minority IPO sales reduce the government's holding without handing over control."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "The government sells a portion of its holding in a listed PSU through the stock exchange's dedicated window for promoters. This is called:", ["Qualified institutions placement", "Rights issue", "Offer for sale through the exchange", "Preferential allotment"], 2, "The OFS mechanism lets promoters, including the government, sell shares of listed companies through a separate exchange window. QIPs, rights issues and preferential allotments involve the company issuing fresh shares."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "Which of the following must be disclosed as a risk factor if material?", ["The name of the advertising agency for the issue", "The colour scheme of the company's logo", "The personal hobbies of the company's directors", "Pending litigation that could hurt operations"], 3, "Material litigation, regulatory actions and business risks must be disclosed so investors can assess the issue. Trivial details such as advertising agencies or logos are not risk factors."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "Which is the correct order of these IPO steps?", ["Bidding, filing of DRHP, allotment, SEBI observations, filing of RHP", "Filing of RHP, bidding, filing of DRHP, SEBI observations, allotment", "Filing of DRHP, SEBI observations, filing of RHP, bidding, allotment", "SEBI observations, filing of DRHP, allotment, bidding, filing of RHP"], 2, "The draft is filed with SEBI first, SEBI gives observations, the RHP is filed with the RoC, bidding opens, and allotment follows issue closure. Bidding cannot precede the offer document."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "In due diligence, which approach reflects a merchant banker's proper standard?", ["Independently verifying key claims made by management", "Copying management's statements into the offer document without checks", "Relying only on the previous year's newspaper reports", "Verifying information only if SEBI specifically asks for it"], 0, "Due diligence requires reasonable independent verification through documents, site visits, legal and financial checks and confirmations. Mere reproduction of management claims does not meet the standard expected of a lead manager."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "A company's paid-up capital is ₹200 crore and its free reserves are ₹800 crore. What is the maximum buyback it can make with only board approval?", ["₹250 crore", "₹200 crore", "₹100 crore", "₹80 crore"], 2, "With board approval alone, a buyback is limited to 10% of paid-up capital plus free reserves: 10% × (₹200 + ₹800) crore = ₹100 crore. A special resolution allows up to 25%, which would be ₹250 crore."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "Which of the following services would a merchant banker typically NOT provide?", ["Managing public issues of securities", "Advising on mergers and takeovers", "Accepting public deposits like a commercial bank", "Underwriting issues of securities"], 2, "Accepting deposits from the public is a banking activity regulated by the RBI. Issue management, M&A advisory and underwriting are core merchant banking activities under SEBI regulations."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "A merchant banker receives an order from SEBI imposing a penalty. If aggrieved, it may appeal to:", ["The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission", "The Securities Appellate Tribunal", "The Insurance Ombudsman", "The Reserve Bank of India"], 1, "Appeals against SEBI orders lie with the Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT), and further to the Supreme Court. Consumer forums, the Insurance Ombudsman and RBI do not hear appeals against SEBI orders."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "A company's post-issue paid-up equity capital will be ₹50 crore. What is the minimum promoter contribution required in its IPO under ICDR?", ["₹5 crore", "₹12.5 crore", "₹25 crore", "₹10 crore"], 3, "Promoters must contribute at least 20% of the post-issue capital: 20% × ₹50 crore = ₹10 crore. ₹12.5 crore would be 25%, which reflects confusion with the minimum public shareholding norm."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "Under UPI-based ASBA, what happens to a retail investor's money when he bids?", ["The amount is blocked in his bank account until allotment is finalised", "The amount is debited and paid to the issuer immediately", "The amount is transferred to the lead manager's own account", "Nothing happens until shares are listed"], 0, "Under ASBA, funds stay in the investor's account but are blocked through a UPI mandate. On allotment, only the amount for allotted shares is debited and the rest unblocked; there is no transfer to the lead manager."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "In a firm underwriting arrangement, the underwriter:", ["Subscribes only if the issue is fully subscribed by the public", "Has no obligation if the issue fails", "Is paid only if the shares list at a premium", "Commits to take a fixed number of shares in any case"], 3, "In firm underwriting, the underwriter commits to take up a specified number of securities irrespective of subscription by the public, in addition to any devolvement obligation. Its commission does not depend on listing gains."], ["Disinvestment & Buyback", "Under the current tax rules effective from October 2024, money received by a resident shareholder on a buyback of shares is:", ["Fully exempt in the hands of the shareholder", "Treated as a dividend and taxed in the shareholder's hands", "Taxed only in the company's hands at a flat rate", "Taxed as long-term capital gain at 10%"], 1, "From 1 October 2024, buyback proceeds are treated as deemed dividend taxable in the shareholder's hands, with the cost of shares treated as a capital loss. Earlier, the company paid buyback tax and holders were exempt."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "Which of the following is a debt instrument commonly issued by companies to raise long-term funds?", ["Equity shares with voting rights", "Sweat equity shares", "Units of an equity mutual fund", "Non-convertible debentures"], 3, "Non-convertible debentures are debt securities that pay interest and are repaid at maturity. Equity and sweat equity shares represent ownership, and mutual fund units are issued by funds, not companies borrowing money."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "A lead manager learns of a material adverse development about the issuer after the RHP is filed but before bidding closes. It should:", ["Ignore it, since the RHP has already been filed", "Ensure it is disclosed before the issue closes", "Disclose it only in the next annual report", "Inform only anchor investors privately"], 1, "The lead manager must ensure investors receive updated material information, typically through an addendum or public notice, before they commit. Selective disclosure to anchors would be unfair and violate disclosure norms."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "A company's shares change hands between two investors on a stock exchange. What does the company receive from this trade?", ["The full sale value of the shares", "The difference between issue price and sale price", "A fee equal to the stamp duty paid", "Nothing, as the trade is between investors"], 3, "Secondary market trades transfer existing shares between investors; the issuer receives no money. Companies raise funds only when they issue securities in the primary market."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "For how long must a merchant banker generally preserve books of account, records and documents?", ["Only until the issue gets listed", "Only for the current financial year", "Until the next SEBI inspection is completed", "For the minimum period set by SEBI regulations"], 3, "Merchant bankers must maintain prescribed books and records and preserve them for the minimum period laid down in the regulations, so SEBI can inspect past issues. Discarding records after listing or one year would breach this."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "Bidders applying for more than ₹2 lakh in a public issue who are not QIBs fall under the category of:", ["Retail individual investors", "Anchor investors", "Qualified institutional buyers", "Non-institutional investors"], 3, "Applicants who are not QIBs and bid above ₹2 lakh are non-institutional investors (NIIs). Retail individual investors bid up to ₹2 lakh, while anchors and QIBs are institutional categories."], ["Registration, Code of Conduct & Obligations", "A merchant banker's employee prepares marketing material claiming the IPO 'will surely list at a 50% premium'. Under the code of conduct this is:", ["Permitted if the grey market premium supports it", "Permitted if the issuer approves the statement", "Not permitted, as it is a misleading assurance of returns", "Permitted for issues reserved for retail investors"], 2, "The code of conduct prohibits misleading statements and assurances of returns or listing gains. Grey market indications or issuer approval do not make such promises acceptable."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "Which activity of a merchant banker involves arranging finance for a large project from a group of lenders?", ["Portfolio management for retail clients", "Loan syndication", "Market making in SME shares", "Credit rating of the project's bonds"], 1, "Loan syndication means bringing together several banks or institutions to jointly lend to a project. Portfolio management and credit rating are separate regulated activities, and market making is a trading function in SME issues."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "In a public issue with several lead managers, how is the work usually divided?", ["Only one lead manager is allowed to sign the due diligence certificate", "SEBI assigns each lead manager to a different class of investors", "The registrar decides which lead manager handles which task", "Their responsibilities are allocated and disclosed"], 3, "When more than one lead manager is appointed, the inter se allocation of responsibilities is set out and disclosed in the offer document, so accountability for each activity is clear. All lead managers remain responsible for due diligence."], ["Introduction to Capital Market", "A company issues new shares to existing shareholders in proportion to their holdings, at a price usually below market. This is a:", ["Rights issue", "Preferential allotment to a strategic investor", "Initial public offer on the main board", "Offer for sale by promoters"], 0, "A rights issue offers shares to existing shareholders pro rata, often at a discount, and the rights can usually be renounced. A preferential allotment goes to selected persons, and an offer for sale involves existing shares."], ["Issue Management – Process & Underwriting", "An issuer revises the price band during the bidding period. What must it do as a result?", ["Extend the bidding period by some working days", "Cancel all bids already received and invite fresh bids", "Refile the draft offer document with SEBI", "Reduce the issue size by half"], 0, "On revision of the price band, the bid period must be extended by at least three working days, subject to the overall maximum, with wide publicity. Existing bids are not cancelled and refiling is not required."], ["Issue Management – Obligations & Due Diligence", "Issue advertisements prepared by the issuer and lead manager must:", ["Highlight expected listing gains to attract investors", "Mention only the positive aspects of the issuer", "Be truthful, fair and consistent with the offer document", "Avoid any reference to risk factors"], 2, "ICDR advertisement norms require advertisements to be fair, not misleading, and consistent with the offer document; they cannot promise returns or omit material risks. Selective positive messaging would mislead investors."], ["Introduction to Merchant Banking", "Why is a merchant banker described as a 'gatekeeper' of the primary market?", ["Its due diligence screens issues before they reach investors", "It decides which investors may trade in the secondary market", "It fixes the listing fee charged by stock exchanges", "It guarantees a minimum listing-day gain for investors"], 0, "Merchant bankers verify disclosures and compliance before an issue is launched, acting as a first line of protection for investors. They do not control secondary trading, set exchange fees or guarantee returns."], ["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "A listed company has 10 crore shares. An acquirer crosses the 25% threshold and must make a mandatory open offer for the minimum size. How many shares must the offer cover?", ["2.5 crore shares", "1.0 crore shares", "5.1 crore shares", "2.6 crore shares"], 3, "The minimum open offer size is 26% of the total shares: 26% × 10 crore = 2.6 crore shares. 2.5 crore reflects the trigger level, not the offer size."], ["Issue Management – Important Terms", "In a book-built issue, what does 'cut-off price' mean for a retail bidder?", ["Bidding at the floor price only", "Bidding at a price below the floor price", "Withdrawing the bid if the price is above the floor", "Accepting the final price discovered within the band"], 3, "A cut-off bid means the investor accepts the final issue price determined within the band. Such bidders block funds at the cap price so that their bid remains valid whatever the final price."], ["Mergers, Acquisitions & Takeovers", "Which of the following can trigger an open offer obligation under SAST even without crossing a shareholding threshold?", ["Purchase of 1% of shares by a mutual fund", "Receipt of a bonus issue by an existing holder", "Grant of ESOPs to employees", "Acquisition of control over the target company"], 3, "SAST requires an open offer when an acquirer acquires control, directly or indirectly, regardless of the percentage acquired. Small purchases, bonus issues and ESOP grants do not by themselves trigger an open offer."]]};
